From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usbprint.sys, wineusb.sys: a USB printer is a device its maker's software can open

Printer makers' Windows software talks to its USB printer itself: DYMO
Connect finds a LabelWriter 550 among the GUID_DEVINTERFACE_USBPRINT
interfaces, reads its IEEE 1284 ID, writes a status request (ESC A) on one
overlapped handle and reads the 32-byte status on another -- the loaded
roll, the labels left. Wine had no usbprint.sys; the printer was "not
connected".

usbprint.sys is our own driver for USB printer-class interfaces (class 7,
subclass 1; usbprint.inf binds USB\Class_07&SubClass_01): the interface
GUID_DEVINTERFACE_USBPRINT, IOCTL_USBPRINT_GET_1284_ID / GET_LPT_STATUS /
SOFT_RESET / VENDOR_SET_COMMAND / VENDOR_GET_COMMAND / GET/SET_PROTOCOL, reads and
writes on the bulk pipes (a read returns what came within 5 seconds, as the
printer answers or not). It makes the Printer-class device a printer is on
Windows, USBPRINT\<MFG+MDL, 20 characters><checksum>, compatible ID
GENPRINTER, its instance the printer's serial number as CUPS takes it (the
1284 ID's SERIALNUMBER, SERN or SN, else the USB one; USB001 without one),
described by usbprinter.inf. Nothing is held while no program has the
printer open: the last handle closed gives the interface back.

wineusb.sys: on Linux usblp has a printer's interface, and usbfs refused
every transfer on it -- the submit failed, and the IRP was left pending for
good. The interface is now claimed before a transfer on it, the kernel driver
detached for the while (as CUPS's usb backend does), and released when the
device is unconfigured; a transfer libusb cannot submit fails its IRP.
URB_FUNCTION_CLASS_DEVICE and CLASS_INTERFACE reach the device (the printer
class's GET_DEVICE_ID and GET_PORT_STATUS are class requests).

Test: test/usbprint-gate.sh -- a libusb-1.0 of the gate's own with one USB
printer on it (test/usbprint-fakeusb.c, its interface held by a kernel driver
as usblp holds it), DYMO Connect's walk in test/usbprint-probe.c, 64- and
32-bit (mutants SG_MUTANT_USBCLAIM, SG_MUTANT_USBPRINT_SHARE).
